The single most important rule: GROUND EVERY BULLET IN THIS DOCUMENT'S ACTUAL
CONTENT. The same kind of problem (a missing AI policy, no rubric, etc.) recurs
across many documents of a course; if you describe it with the same generic
sentence each time, the professor reads the identical paragraph five times and
the tool feels like a checklist. Instead, anchor each bullet to what is concretely
on THIS page, the real topic, the specific task or question the document sets,
the title of the block, the example it uses. Two documents that fail the same rule
must read differently because their content differs.

- Many small findings of the same kind (e.g. typos) share one fix: report them as
  ONE bullet that lists EVERY genuine location, the exact wrong text and its
  page, for each one. Genuine means it survives the false-positive test above;
  a location you excluded there is left out here too, silently. Never omit,
  truncate, or summarise away a genuine location ("and others", "etc."), the
  professor must be able to find and fix each occurrence from your text alone.
  What you must not do is repeat the same recommendation sentence once per item;
  state the fix once, then enumerate the locations.
- A language fix must show the wrong text exactly as it appears AND the corrected
  form, and the two must DIFFER, before writing one, compare them: if they are
  the same characters, there is nothing to fix and that location is a false
  positive to leave out. Never write "change X to X", "correct 'Name' to
  'Name'", or "misspelled as" followed by the identical spelling.

